# Rust Stabilization Rules

## Operating Principle

During v0.3 stabilization, enforce direction without normalizing legacy debt:
do not add new violations, and migrate touched code toward the standard when the
change is local and testable.
Do not expand stabilization PRs into broad maintainability refactors unless that
is the block objective.

## Maintainability

- Prefer small, focused functions over large orchestration blocks.
- Do not grow `lakehouse/mod.rs`; when touching it, split HTTP bootstrap,
  signing, or init orchestration into focused modules instead.
- Avoid boolean parameters for behavior selection; prefer enums or separate functions.
- Keep trait interfaces narrow and boundary-oriented.
- Keep visibility minimal: private, then `pub(crate)`, then `pub`.
- Encapsulate invariants behind constructors and narrow public methods.
- Comments explain invariants, tradeoffs, safety, or non-obvious behavior — not
  restatements of the code.
- No TODO/FIXME without reason, owner, or tracking reference.

## Error Boundaries

- Treat filesystem, object-store, catalog, subprocess, crypto, and config
  operations as fallible.
- Do not introduce new `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` on runtime I/O, catalog,
  object-store, subprocess, or crypto paths.
- `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` OK in `#[cfg(test)]` when they keep tests clear.
- Static invariant `.expect()` in `LazyLock::new` (or equivalent) OK with a
  clear message.
- `anyhow::Result` OK in `main.rs`, `cli/`, and top-level command runners
  (`ingest::run`, `scan::run`, `profile::run`, `query::run`).
- Library internals (`domain/`, `storage/`, `lakehouse/schema.rs`) prefer typed
  errors when touched. Do not expand broad `anyhow` without reason.
- At command boundaries, attach `.context` naming the operation and resource
  (path, bucket, table, endpoint).

## Boundaries And Secrets

- Keep domain logic separate from CLI, I/O, subprocess, catalog, and object-store
  execution where practical.
- Prefer typed domain values over raw `String` / `bool` / `PathBuf` across layers.
- Object-store read/write/list/head/delete via OpenDAL (`storage::create_operator`).
  Details: `.cursor/rules/project-architecture.mdc`.
- Direct HTTP only for Lakekeeper bootstrap, doctor/health, ingest preflight,
  and narrow signed setup. Keep fallible.
- Never put secret fields on `Debug` / `Display`. Credentials must not appear in
  logs, CLI dumps, or `{:?}` of config structs. Prefer redacted types when touched.
- Catalog/store consistency failures are command-level failures unless an
  explicit partial-success policy is documented and tested.

## Exit And Partial Success

- Mutating commands with per-item failures must return non-success unless an
  explicit partial-success exit policy is documented and tested.
- Do not treat “completed with errors” + exit 0 as the desired default when
  touching ingest/scan exit semantics.
- Non-dry-run ingest/query should preflight object store and catalog, or
  document the gap when changing connectivity checks.

## Async Safety

- No blocking work on the async runtime: no `std::fs::*`, sync `WalkDir`, or
  sync `std::process::Command` inside `async fn` without `spawn_blocking` (or
  tokio process APIs).
- CPU-heavy work (hashing, compression): `spawn_blocking`.
- Object-store and catalog-backed ingest/query paths need explicit timeouts
  (OpenDAL layers and/or request budgets), not only HTTP health clients.
- External tools (`exiftool`, `ffprobe`, `pdfinfo`) need bounded execution
  before being described as hardened.
- Do not hold mutex / RwLock / RefCell guards across `.await`.

## CLI Contract

- No new inert flags or placeholder success semantics.
- Every public CLI flag needs runtime behavior, tests, and docs — or hide/remove
  before calling it stable.
- Placeholder commands must return non-success once exit semantics are touched.
- Mutating ops need `--dry-run` or an explicit “not implemented yet” statement.
- CLI help, runtime behavior, tests, and docs must agree for changed commands.

## Legacy Debt

- No broad `#[allow(dead_code)]` without inline rationale.
- No new `println!` / `eprintln!` in reusable library-style modules. Operator UX
  may print; lakehouse I/O diagnostics should use `tracing` when touched.
- Do not migrate existing `println!` unless that migration is the block objective.
- Avoid unnecessary `.clone()` in hot paths, but do not contort simple code for
  cheap clones.

## Dependencies

- Check `Cargo.toml` before adding external `use`s.
- Prefer `std`, then existing crates, then Ask first for heavy additions.
- Remove unused crates when touched, or document why they stay.

## Testing

- Unit-test pure logic in `domain/`, `config/`, `lakehouse/schema.rs`.
- Container-backed integration tests are planned (S2). Do not add testcontainers
  until accepted.
- Until then: keep `#[ignore]` stack tests named and documented; do not claim
  end-to-end coverage from unit tests alone.
- Use `tempfile`. No hardcoded machine paths.
- `#[tokio::test]` for async; `#[test]` for sync.
- Positive and negative assertions for changed user-facing behavior.
